page guide_scripting {
  title = "Guest automation with wscript"
  chapter_header "Guest automation with wscript" {
    kicker       = "The guide · explanation"
    reading_time = "12 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<'DOC'
Every script vmlab runs is written in **wscript**, a small statically typed
language with Rust-shaped syntax. vmlab registers its lab, machine and segment
API as a wscript host module, so a provision script, an event handler and an
ad-hoc `vmlab script` run all see the same surface. Scripts are
daemon-unaware: they receive a `Lab` handle and operate on it, and every
blocking call takes a timeout and returns a `Result`. This chapter explains the
language in brief, the three handles, where scripts run from, and how errors
travel. The exact signatures are in the reference:
[Lab and Segment](ref_ws_lab), [Machine](ref_ws_machine), and
[Term, types and the vmlab module](ref_ws_types).
DOC

  h2 "The language in brief"

  p <<'DOC'
wscript is statically typed with local inference: you annotate function
signatures and nothing else, and a type error, including a wrong argument to a
vmlab method, is reported when the script compiles rather than when it runs.
`vmlab validate` compiles every script the lab file references, so a typo in a
method name fails before any machine boots (see [The lab file](guide_lab_file)).
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
The types you meet most are `string`, `int`, `float`, `bool`, `unit`,
`List[T]`, `Option[T]` and `Result[T, E]`. Statements end at newlines, so no
semicolons are needed. `if` and `match` are expressions, loops are `for x in
xs`, `for i in 0..n`, `while` and `loop`, and `fmt("{} ready", name)` builds a
string. Strings carry the usual methods: `trim`, `split`, `contains`,
`to_lower`, `ends_with`.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
A script names the host module with `use vmlab` at the top. That brings the
`Lab`, `Machine`, `Segment` and `Term` types into scope, along with the
`vmlab::` module functions: `vmlab::sleep_ms(ms)` pauses the script, and
`vmlab::env(name)` reads a host environment variable, returning an empty string
when it is unset. The older name `Vm` still compiles as a silent alias for
`Machine`, so first-boot scripts embedded in earlier templates keep working.
DOC

  h3 "Result handling"

  p <<'DOC'
Every call that can fail returns `Result[T, string]`, where the error is a
message naming the machine and the reason. There are three ways to consume one,
and the shipped examples use all three:
DOC

  list {
    li "`?` propagates the error out of the current function. The function must itself return a `Result`, which is why most examples put their work in a `setup(lab) -> Result[unit, string]` and call it from `main`."
    li "`match r { Ok(v) => …, Err(e) => … }` handles both arms in place. `match` is exhaustive, so a missing arm is a compile error."
    li "`let Ok(vm) = lab.vm(\"dc01\") else { return }` binds the success value or leaves the function. `let Some(x) = opt else { … }` does the same for an `Option`."
  }

  p <<'DOC'
`r.expect("message")` and `r.unwrap()` take the value or raise a fault that
ends the script. The examples use `expect` in `main` to turn a failed setup
into a failed provision with a readable message. A value you do not use must
still be bound, so a fire-and-forget call reads `let k = m.send_keys("enter")`.
DOC

  code ws { filename = "scripts/setup.ws" source = <<'SRC'
use vmlab

fn setup(lab: Lab) -> Result[unit, string] {
    let web = lab.vm("web")?
    web.wait_ready(600)?
    let r = web.exec_timeout("/bin/sh", ["-c", "apt-get install -y nginx"], 600)?
    if r.exit_code != 0 {
        return Err("nginx install failed: " + r.stderr)
    }
    lab.log("nginx installed on " + web.ip()?)
    Ok(())
}

fn main(lab: Lab) {
    setup(lab).expect("setup failed")
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "The host module and its three handles"

  p <<'DOC'
The module exposes three opaque handle types. All state rides inside them, so
the same module serves compile-checking and live execution.
DOC

  h3 "Lab"

  p <<'DOC'
`Lab` is what every entry point receives. It answers `name()`, writes to the lab
log and the live CLI stream with `log(msg)`, and hands out the other two
handles. `lab.machine(name)` returns any machine; `lab.vm(name)` and
`lab.container(name)` return the same handle but refuse the other kind with a
message that says which it is, which is a better error than "no such
machine". `machines()`, `vms()` and `containers()` list them, and
`lab.segment(name)` returns a segment handle or an error naming the lab.
DOC

  h3 "Machine"

  p <<'DOC'
There is one `Machine` handle for a VM and a container alike. Every operation
is available on every machine, and what a particular machine cannot do is
reported at call time by naming the capability: `screenshot` on a machine with
no display fails with "machine `api` has no display", never with "no such
method" and never with a claim about its kind. The methods fall into six
groups: lifecycle and state (`start`, `stop`, `stop_force`, `restart`,
`poweroff`, `state`, `wait_ready`, `is_ready`, `is_healthy`,
`agent_answering`, `wait_shutdown`, `ip`, `ip_nic`, `logs`), snapshots
(`snapshot`, `restore`, `restore_discarding_workspace`, `snapshots`,
`delete_snapshot`), input and screen (covered in
[Screens, input and vision](guide_vision)), the guest agent (`exec`,
`exec_timeout`, `copy_to`, `copy_from`, `terminal`, `stats`), and identity
(`logins`, `as_login`, `as_account`, covered in
[Logins and the SSH facade](guide_logins)).
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
`poweroff` is a clean QMP quit that flushes block caches first, unlike
`stop_force`, which kills QEMU. For a guest with no ACPI it is the only way to
seal a consistent disk. Snapshot calls go through the lab runtime so the
records, events and pin-guarding stay in one place; see
[Snapshots](guide_snapshots) for what a restore does to a dev machine's
workspace.
DOC

  h3 "Segment"

  p <<'DOC'
`Segment` mutates a segment's network services at runtime: `dns_set`,
`dns_sinkhole` and `dns_clear` edit the segment's DNS zone, `block`,
`block_port`, `unblock` and `redirect` edit its L3 rules, `forward` adds a
host port forward to a machine's leased address, and `rules()` returns the
current rule set as JSON. Each rule-adding call returns a rule id the removing
call takes back. `route_to` and `unroute_to` exist in the interface but
currently return an error saying inter-segment routing is not yet available
from scripts. See [Networking](guide_networking) for what these rules do.
DOC

  h2 "Where a script runs from"

  p <<'DOC'
A **provision script** is declared with a `provision "path.ws" { }` block
inside the `vm {}` or `container {}` it configures. Its entry point is
`fn main(lab: Lab)`. It runs once during `vmlab up`, after that machine is
ready, at its position among the machine's steps, interleaved with the
machine's [playbooks](guide_playbooks) in declaration order. Across machines,
steps follow the order the machine blocks appear, with `depends_on` gating
when each becomes eligible. A script orchestrating several machines is the
normal case: it reaches the others through the lab handle.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
`lab.this_vm()` returns the machine whose `provision {}` block declared the
running script, or the build VM when a template's first-boot script runs. It
is an error from an event handler or from `vmlab script`, which have no owning
machine. Inside a machine's **own first-boot provision**, `is_ready` and
`wait_ready` mean agent-level readiness rather than full readiness, because
full readiness is unreachable until that script returns; a first-boot script
that reboots its guest uses them to wait for it to come back. Everywhere else
they mean full readiness. `agent_answering` is the ungated live probe and goes
false while a guest is down or mid-reboot.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
An **ad-hoc run** is `vmlab script scripts/whatever.ws`, given a path relative
to the lab root. It uses the same `main(lab)` entry point against the running
lab, with no owning machine. See [vmlab script](cmd_script).
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
An **event handler** has the entry point `fn handle(event: Event, lab: Lab)`
and is bound with an `on {}` block. Handler failures are logged and never
fatal. See [Events and handlers](guide_events).
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
A **template build script** gets the same API scoped to the single build VM.
See [Templates and the store](guide_templates).
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Relative local paths in a script resolve against the script's own directory,
not the lab root: a `copy_to("scripts/editor-bits.ps1", …)` from
`scripts/editor-bits.ws` reads `scripts/scripts/editor-bits.ps1`. This is what
lets a provision ship reference images and payload files beside itself, and it
holds for template builds, which run from a separate working directory.
DOC

  h2 "Exec, copy and terminal"

  p <<'DOC'
`m.exec(cmd, args)` runs a program in the guest over the agent channel with a
120 second timeout; `m.exec_timeout(cmd, args, secs)` sets the timeout. Both
return an `ExecResult` carrying `exit_code`, `stdout` and `stderr`. The command
is an argv, not a shell line, so a pipeline needs an explicit
`"/bin/sh", ["-c", "…"]` or `"cmd.exe", ["/c", "…"]`. Output is streamed and
captured, not polled. A guest built from a template with no agent has no exec
transport at all.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
`m.copy_to(local, guest_path)` and `m.copy_from(guest_path, local)` move one
file each way over the agent's file session, with a guest-computed digest
verifying the transfer. `copy_from` creates the local parent directory. Both
run as the handle's identity, which is the agent identity unless the handle
came from `as_login` or `as_account`.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
`m.terminal()` opens an interactive shell session and returns a `Term` handle
driven send/expect style. `send_line(text)` sends the text plus a carriage
return, which is what a PTY expects from Enter on both POSIX shells and
PowerShell; `send(text)` sends raw bytes. `expect(pattern, secs)` waits until
the accumulated output matches a regex, consumes through the end of the match
and returns what it consumed, so successive expects walk the stream; on timeout
the error carries the unmatched tail. `read()` drains whatever is queued,
`resize(cols, rows)` resizes the PTY, and `close()` ends the session. The shell
sees a real PTY, so prompts, echoes and ANSI sequences are all in the buffer.
DOC

  code ws { source = <<'SRC'
let t = vm.terminal()?
let s = t.send_line("hostname")
let out = t.expect("box", 10)?
t.close()
SRC
  }

  p <<'DOC'
`m.stats()` returns a `GuestStats` sample from the agent's metrics feature:
CPU percent, memory used and total, and a `DiskStat` per mounted filesystem.
`m.logs(lines)` returns the machine's console log where it keeps one, a
container's captured stdout and stderr or a VM's serial log.
DOC

  h2 "The interface file and the LSP"

  p <<'DOC'
vmlab ships its API as a `.wscripti` interface file so the wscript language
server can give diagnostics, hover and completion for lab scripts. The hidden
verb `vmlab __wscripti [path]` writes it, defaulting to `vmlab.wscripti` in
the current directory. List that file in a `wscript.toml` beside your scripts;
`wscript check` and `wscript lsp` read it from there. The file is generated
and says so in its header; do not edit it by hand.
DOC

  callout "Templates record the surface they were built against" {
    kind = :note
    body = "A template stores the wscript surface version its scripts compiled against. A template below this host's supported floor is refused with one message telling you to rebuild it with this vmlab version, rather than a cascade of compiler errors."
  }

  h2 "Error handling"

  p <<'DOC'
A compile error names the file, the message, the diagnostic code and any help
text. A runtime error out of a provision script carries the message plus the
call chain it surfaced through, is written to the lab log as
"script failed: …", and fails the provision run, which fails `vmlab up`. An
error inside a handler is logged with a warning and nothing else stops. A
method error is always a string you can log or match on, and it names the
machine: a timed-out `wait_for_image` says how long it waited and on which
machine, and a `terminal` whose shell exited says so and quotes the tail of
its output.
DOC

  p <<'DOC'
Idempotence is the script author's job. Every `vmlab up` runs the provision
steps of each machine it brings into scope, whether that machine was just
cloned or was already running, so a script that must not repeat a step checks
the guest first. The mixed-lab example reads a registry value before enabling
autologon and skips the reboot when it is already set; see
[Example labs](apx_examples).
DOC
}
